course: biology
lesson: 223
title: Gulls: chip thieves, rain dances and salty water
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down and snatches one right out of a child's hand: gulls are bold and clever, and they have learned that people carry food.
explain: On a park lawn a gull may stamp its feet up and down like a little dance; the drumming feels like rain to the worms, they come up, and the gull eats them.
explain: Gulls can drink sea water, because glands above their eyes take the salt out of their blood, and the salty water drips out of their nose.
explain: There is no single bird called a seagull: there are many kinds of gull, like the herring gull and the black-headed gull, and many of them live far inland, around rubbish tips, fields and city roofs.
explain: A brown speckled gull is not a different bird but a young one, and a big herring gull takes about four years to get its grown-up grey and white feathers.
fact: Gulls are bold and clever, and they learn that people carry food, so some snatch chips and sandwiches at the seaside.
fact: A gull stamps its feet on grass to imitate rain, which makes worms come up to the surface where the gull can catch them.
fact: Gulls can drink salt water, because glands above their eyes remove the salt and it drips out of their nose.
fact: Many gulls have moved into towns and nest on flat rooftops, because cities have food and safe roofs away from foxes.
fact: A herring gull chick pecks the red spot on its parent's beak to ask for food, and the pecking makes the parent bring up food for it.
fact: Seagull is just a nickname; there are many kinds of gull, such as herring gulls and black-headed gulls, and many live inland.
fact: A gull drops a shellfish onto rocks from a height to smash the shell open.
fact: Gulls follow a tractor ploughing a field, because ploughing turns up worms and grubs.
fact: Young gulls are brown and speckled; a herring gull takes about four years to look fully grown up.
fact: Herring gulls seem to be everywhere in towns, but in the wild their numbers have fallen, and in Britain they are a protected species.
fact: Gulls have many calls; the loud long call with the head thrown back claims a territory.
fact: A gull has two legs, two wings and webbed feet for swimming.
exercise: Why do gulls snatch chips at the seaside? | They are bold and have learned people carry food | They are so hungry they will die without chips | They hate people and want to scare them off the beach | They think the chips are fish
exercise: Why does a gull stamp its feet on the grass? | It feels like rain, so worms come up | It is warming its cold feet | It is dancing to attract a mate | It is squashing the grass for a nest
exercise: How can a gull drink sea water? | Glands above its eyes remove the salt | It spits the salt out of its beak | Its feathers soak up all the salt | It cannot, so it only ever drinks rain
exercise: Where does the salt go after a gull drinks sea water? | It drips out of its nose | It turns into white feathers | It stays in its stomach for ever | It comes out through its feet
exercise: Why do many gulls nest on city rooftops? | Cities have food and safe roofs | They are afraid of the sea | Rooftops are warmer than eggs | Their parents were all born in zoos
exercise: Why does a gull chick peck the red spot on its parent's beak? | To ask for food, which makes the parent bring it up | To clean its parent's beak | To fight its parent for the best place in the nest | To learn the colour red
exercise: Is there a bird called the seagull? | No, it is a nickname for many kinds of gull | Yes, one kind of gull that lives only far out at sea | Yes, it is a kind of seal with wings | No, seagulls are really pigeons
exercise: Why does a gull drop a shellfish onto the rocks? | To smash the shell open | To wash the sand off it first | To feed it to the hungry crabs | To play a game with other gulls
exercise: Why do gulls follow a ploughing tractor? | Ploughing turns up worms and grubs | They like the loud noise of the engine | They want to ride on the back of the tractor | The farmer feeds them chips
exercise: What is a big brown speckled gull most likely to be? | A young gull, not yet in adult feathers | A gull that has rolled about in the mud | A different bird called a brown gull | A very old gull losing its colour
exercise: How long does a herring gull take to look fully grown up? | About four years | About four short weeks | About one single day | About forty years
exercise: Are herring gulls doing well everywhere? | No, wild numbers have fallen and they are protected in Britain | Yes, there are more than ever everywhere | Yes, they are pests that nobody needs to protect anywhere | No, they died out years ago
exercise: What does a gull's loud long call do? | It claims its territory | It calls fish to the surface | It scares rain clouds away | It means the gull is ill
exercise: What do a gull's webbed feet help it do? | Swim on the water | Hold chips while flying | Climb up tall trees | Dig holes in the sand
test: «gull just nicked my chips right outta my hand, cheeky sod» What explains this? | Gulls are bold and learn that people carry food | Gulls are always starving at the seaside | The gull was trying to save the child from salt | Gulls only steal on Sundays
test: In an animated fish film the gulls all scream «Mine! Mine!» and grab everything. What is true about real gulls? | They are bold and quick to grab food, but they use many different calls | They can really speak human words | They never steal food from anybody | They only eat seaweed and never touch chips
test: «gull's doing a funny tap dance on the footy pitch» What is it most likely doing? | Stamping to bring worms up, like rain | Dancing to music from a car | Trying to break the grass | Waiting for the ball to land so it can peck it
test: What would happen if a gull chick could not see any red spot on its parent's beak? | It would not know where to peck to ask for food | It would find food by itself at once | It would fly off to the sea | It would slowly turn red itself to make up for it
test: «my dad says there's no such bird as a seagull lol is he mad» | He is right; there are many kinds of gull, and seagull is a nickname | He is wrong; the seagull is the only kind of gull | He is wrong; gulls live only out in the sea | He is right; all gulls are really ducks
test: Count them: 5 gulls stand on a lamppost and 3 more land. How many legs now? | 16 | 10 | 8 | 32
test: A boy sees a brown speckled gull next to a grey and white one and says they are different birds. What is right? | The brown one is probably a young gull of the same kind | He is right, brown gulls never turn grey | The brown one has been in a fire and its feathers got burnt | The grey one is a pigeon
test: What would a gull do if it found a mussel, a shellfish, too hard to open with its beak? | Fly up and drop it onto rocks | Give up eating shellfish for good | Wait for the shell to open by itself | Swallow it whole and spit out the shell
test: «gulls everywhere in my town, why's anyone protect them» What is true? | In the wild, herring gull numbers have fallen | Gulls are protected because they are rare in towns | Nobody protects gulls anywhere | Gulls protect themselves, not people
test: Count them: a gull family on a roof has 2 parents and 3 chicks. How many wings altogether? | 10 | 5 | 6 | 20
test: «why's the gull drinking from the sea, won't it get sick??» | No, glands above its eyes take the salt out | Yes, it will die from the salt | No, sea water is not really salty near the beach | Yes, so it spits it all back out
exam: Identify the means by which the gull is enabled to consume sea water without harm. | Glands situated above the eyes remove the salt, which is discharged through the nostrils | The salt is retained within the stomach and is never absorbed | The feathers absorb the salt from the water before it is swallowed | The salt is expelled exclusively through the feet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term seagull? | It is a popular name applied to numerous species of gull, many of which reside inland | It is the scientific name of one species confined to the open ocean | It is the name of a hybrid between a gull and a pigeon | It refers only to the young of the herring gull
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the stamping of the feet by gulls upon grass. | The vibration imitates rainfall and causes worms to emerge | The action serves to warm the feet in cold weather | The action constitutes a courtship display only | The action is intended to flatten vegetation for the purpose of nesting
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a brown and speckled gull is commonly observed alongside grey and white adults. | It is an immature bird which has not yet acquired adult plumage | It is a separate species which never acquires grey plumage | It is an adult bird whose feathers have been soiled with mud | It is an elderly bird whose plumage has faded with advancing age
